Guard the <linux/scc.h> include, which Linux 7.2 removed.

compiler-rt's sanitizer_common records the size of every structure a Linux
ioctl can be handed, so that an interceptor can check the buffer a caller
passed. Two of those belong to the AX.25 SCC drivers, and it reads them from
<linux/scc.h>, unconditionally, on every Linux target that is not Android.

Linux 7.2 removed that header from the UAPI along with the drivers it
described, so against this tree's kernel headers the file does not compile at
all:

    sanitizer_platform_limits_posix.cpp:154:10: fatal error:
        'linux/scc.h' file not found

Nothing else in compiler-rt reads struct_scc_modem_sz or struct_scc_stat_sz --
they are declared, defined, and referenced by no interceptor -- so the two
sizes cost nothing to lose on a kernel that has no such ioctl to intercept.
They are kept where the header is still there, because a runtime that silently
knows less than it did on the kernels it used to cover is the worse trade.

__has_include rather than a version test: the question is whether this header
exists on the host being built against, and nothing here knows which kernel
that is. The zero fallback is the shape the file already uses for
struct_input_keymap_entry_sz on a too-old <linux/input.h>, so the declarations
in sanitizer_platform_limits_posix.h stay unconditional and nothing that links
against them has to know.

Upstreamable as-is, and still open against LLVM: 21.1.2 includes the header
exactly as 18.1.8 does.

--- a/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform_limits_posix.cpp
+++ b/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform_limits_posix.cpp
@@ -151,7 +151,13 @@
 #include <linux/lp.h>
 #include <linux/mroute.h>
 #include <linux/mroute6.h>
+// Linux 7.2 removed <linux/scc.h> from the UAPI along with the AX.25 SCC
+// drivers it described. It is unconditional here, so a build against any
+// kernel's headers from that release on stops at this line before it compiles
+// anything.
+#if __has_include(<linux/scc.h>)
 #include <linux/scc.h>
+#endif
 #include <linux/serial.h>
 #include <sys/msg.h>
 #include <sys/ipc.h>
@@ -520,8 +526,17 @@
   unsigned struct_kbsentry_sz = sizeof(struct kbsentry);
   unsigned struct_mtconfiginfo_sz = sizeof(struct mtconfiginfo);
   unsigned struct_nr_parms_struct_sz = sizeof(struct nr_parms_struct);
+#if __has_include(<linux/scc.h>)
   unsigned struct_scc_modem_sz = sizeof(struct scc_modem);
   unsigned struct_scc_stat_sz = sizeof(struct scc_stat);
+#else
+  // Same shape as struct_input_keymap_entry_sz above when its header is too
+  // old: the symbol stays, so the header needs no #if and nothing that links
+  // against it has to know. Zero is the size of a struct the running kernel
+  // has no ioctl for.
+  unsigned struct_scc_modem_sz = 0;
+  unsigned struct_scc_stat_sz = 0;
+#endif
   unsigned struct_serial_multiport_struct_sz
       = sizeof(struct serial_multiport_struct);
   unsigned struct_serial_struct_sz = sizeof(struct serial_struct);
